    "text": "If total giraffes = 120,000.\nTotal spots = 120,000 * 300 = 36,000,000.\nLet's refine this.\nNumber of giraffes: ~117,000\nAverage spots: a giraffe's surface area is large. An average of 300-400 spots seems reasonable. Let's say 350.\n117,000 * 350 = 40,950,000.\nWait, let's check if 20,874,000 is a reasonable number.\n117,000 * 180 = 21,060,000.\n117,000 * 178 = 20,826,000.\nThis means the threshold they set (20,874,000) corresponds to exactly around 178-179 spots per giraffe.\nIs 178 spots per giraffe the most accurate estimate?\nLet's count spots on a typical giraffe. Many sources say a giraffe has around 200 to 400 spots. Reticulated have fewer but larger (maybe 50-100). Masai have a lot (maybe 300+). An average of 250-300 seems very standard.\nIf the average is 300, the total is 117,000 * 300 = 35,100,000.\n\nLet's think carefully about how many spots a giraffe has. A giraffe is roughly 16 feet tall, 10 feet in body length, circumference maybe 8 feet.\nArea: roughly 16 * 8 + 2 * 10 * 8 (roughly) = 288 sq ft. Wait, surface area of a large animal like a giraffe:\nBody: ~10 ft long, ~5 ft circumference = 50 sq ft.\nNeck: ~7 ft long, ~3 ft circumference = 21 sq ft.\nLegs: 4 legs, ~7 ft long, ~1.5 ft circumference = 42 sq ft.\nHead: ~5 sq ft.\nTotal surface area: ~120 sq ft = ~17,000 sq inches.\nSpots size: average 2 inches by 2 inches = 4 sq inches. But they have gaps between spots.\nIf spots cover 50% of the skin, that's 8500 sq inches / 4 sq inches = ~2100 spots?\nWait, spots aren't 2x2 inches on all species.\nReticulated giraffe spots are ~3-4 inches across. They cover the skin entirely (very little gap). So if average spot is 10-12 sq inches, 17000 / 10 = 1700 spots. Wait.\nActually, people have estimated giraffe spots. Some say an average giraffe has about 200-400 spots. Is that true?\nLet's verify: Reticulated giraffe spots are large polygons. If neck and body have ~80-100 spots total, that's one thing. If they have 2000, that's another.\nLet me 